// _tests_/bit/testdecl/mixed.test.bit — CLI-level fixture for `test "name" {
// }` discovery (#4128, SPEC §19, epic #4126), driven through the built
// compiler by `_tests_/bit/clicmd/cases.bit`'s `testDeclCases`, the same way
// `_tests_/bit/testrunfilter/f.test.bit` drives the `--run` filter cases.
//
// THREE properties in one fixture, deliberately, so a regression in any one
// of them fails a case naming it rather than a generic "something broke":
//
//   - THE DECISION THIS TICKET OWNS, enforced rather than merely stated: a
//     `test "..." { }` declaration and a legacy shape-discovered `fn` are
//     BOTH still discovered from the same file. `legacyStillWorks` proves the
//     bare-fn form still works (#4128 chose "support both, deprecate the
//     bare form for #4131's migration" — see SPEC §19); if a later change
//     removes the bare form, this line must become a compile error the
//     fixture is updated to expect, never a silently-skipped test.
//   - ISOLATION (SPEC §19: "each test runs in its own process, so one
//     failure neither hides the others nor aborts the run"), still true for
//     `test` declarations: "first test panics" is declared FIRST — the
//     load-bearing position, matching testgencheck.bit's own convention —
//     and its sibling `test` declaration plus the legacy `fn` must still run
//     and report after it.
//   - `--run` MATCHES THE DISPLAY STRING, not a symbol: "third test also
//     passes" contains a space, which no Bit identifier can, so a run
//     filtered by a pattern containing one can only match by reading the
//     STRING the author wrote (testgen.bit's `testDisplayNameOf`), never the
//     internal `test$<n>` call symbol a `test` declaration actually lowers
//     to.
//
// Declaration order is deliberately NOT dispatch-form order (a `test`
// declaration, then a plain `fn`, then another `test` declaration): the two
// forms lower in ONE interleaved walk (lowerglobal.bit's Pass A,
// testgen.bit's `lowerModuleTopLevelFuncs`) specifically so this order is
// preserved, matching SPEC §19's "test execution order is the order of
// declaration".

test "first test panics" {
  assert(false)
}

fn legacyStillWorks() {
  assert(1 + 1 == 2)
}

test "third test also passes" {
  assert(2 * 3 == 6)
}
